self_contained_problem:
Consider conformal field theories (CFTs) in d-dimensional Euclidean space that are enriched by the presence of a p-dimensional conformal defect (with p < d), including the special case p = d-1 of a boundary (BCFT). Such a defect preserves a subgroup SO(p+1,1) × SO(d-p) of the ambient conformal group SO(d+1,1) and gives rise to two structures: (i) a "bulk" channel in which local operators in the ambient CFT have an operator-product expansion onto operators living on the defect, and (ii) a "defect" channel in which operators on the defect carry their own OPE. Standard open questions in this setting include classifying the conformal defects of a given bulk CFT, computing defect operator spectra and defect-bulk OPE coefficients, understanding boundary/defect entropies and their monotonicity under defect renormalization-group flows, and characterizing the constraints imposed by crossing symmetry on bulk one-point functions and bulk-to-defect two-point functions.

Determine to what extent each of the following techniques can be developed into an effective, systematic tool for answering such structural questions about boundary and defect CFTs:

(a) supersymmetry (e.g., superconformal Ward identities, localization on manifolds with boundary or defects, defect indices);
(b) the numerical and analytic conformal bootstrap, applied to the mixed system of bulk and defect crossing equations;
(c) integrability, in particular for defects in planar N = 4 super Yang–Mills and related integrable models;
(d) topological field theory, including the use of fully extended TQFTs and topological defects to organize symmetry categories and defect data.

Specifically, formulate criteria of "effectiveness" (e.g., yielding rigorous bounds on defect CFT data, exact computations of defect observables, or classification results for conformal defects) and decide for which classes of boundary/defect CFTs each of (a)–(d) meets these criteria.

Consider the second 0⁺ excited state of the ¹²C nucleus (the so-called Hoyle state), located approximately 7.65 MeV above the ground state and just above the 3α breakup threshold. Modern ab initio nuclear-structure approaches — including the no-core Monte Carlo shell model, the symmetry-adapted no-core shell model, and nuclear lattice effective field theory based on chiral interactions evaluated by quantum Monte Carlo methods — have reproduced parts of the low-lying ¹²C spectrum and, in some cases, the excitation energy and monopole transition matrix element M(E0; 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂) of the Hoyle state.

The problem: within each of these ab initio frameworks, compute, for the 0⁺₂ Hoyle state of ¹²C from first-principles nucleon dynamics (without phenomenological cluster assumptions), the following observables and demonstrate quantitative agreement with experiment:
(a) the inelastic electron-scattering form factor F(q) for the 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂ transition as a function of momentum transfer q (in particular reproducing the experimentally observed diffraction structure and magnitude);
(b) the monopole transition probability M(E0; 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂) (equivalently the strength extracted from F(q) at low q);
(c) the root-mean-square matter (or charge) radius of the Hoyle state, which experimentally is significantly larger than that of the ¹²C ground state.

Establish whether each of the listed ab initio methods can simultaneously reproduce (a), (b), and (c), thereby validating it as a correct microscopic theory of the Hoyle state, rather than reproducing only the excitation energy or partial spectroscopic information.

Formulate a common, socially accepted quality-assurance (QA) framework for safety-critical machine-learning systems—such as automated-driving systems whose environmental perception and decision-making rely on machine-learning or deep-learning models—under the following requirements:

1. The framework must specify QA processes (development, verification, validation, deployment monitoring, and update procedures) suitable for systems whose behavior is learned from data rather than fully specified by hand.
2. The framework must integrate and organize open technical problems and their interdependencies across the following fields:
   (a) automobile (or analogous domain) standardization, including functional safety standards;
   (b) statistics, including techniques for data sampling, generalization-error estimation, and uncertainty quantification;
   (c) software engineering, including testing, requirements, configuration management, and lifecycle processes adapted to learned components;
   (d) machine learning, including robustness, interpretability, distribution shift, and validation of trained models;
   (e) theoretical computer science, including formal verification and complexity-theoretic limits of guarantees on learned systems.
3. The framework must be capable of producing technically reasonable, standardizable drafts of QA processes that can serve as a basis for international consensus.

Task: Construct such a framework, or prove obstructions establishing that no single framework satisfying (1)–(3) can be formed under stated assumptions; in either case, exhibit the explicit organization of open problems across the five fields and the interfaces between them.

Consider 10-dimensional type II supergravity (type IIA or type IIB) with localized sources consisting of orientifold Op-planes and/or Dp-branes of a single, common dimension p (so that all sources are mutually parallel, i.e., they all wrap the same internal directions). Adopt the standard smeared ansatz in which:

(i) the 10-dimensional spacetime is a warped product M_4 × M_6, where M_4 is 4-dimensional de Sitter space (with strictly positive cosmological constant) and M_6 is a compact 6-dimensional group manifold (the quotient of a 6-dimensional Lie group by a discrete cocompact subgroup);
(ii) the dilaton is constant, the warp factor is trivial, and the contributions of the Op/Dp sources are smeared (uniformly distributed) over the directions transverse to them inside M_6;
(iii) all NSNS and RR fluxes are taken to be constant in the left-invariant basis of M_6;
(iv) the equations of motion of the bosonic sector of type II supergravity (10-dimensional Einstein equations, dilaton equation, flux Bianchi identities and equations of motion, and source contributions consistent with the appropriate tadpole conditions) are satisfied.

Question: Does there exist any choice of p ∈ {0, 1, …, 8}, of the 6-dimensional group manifold M_6, and of constant fluxes such that the above ansatz yields a bona fide solution with M_4 = dS_4? Equivalently, prove or disprove the conjecture that no classical de Sitter solution of 10-dimensional type II supergravity exists in the parallel-sources, smeared, group-manifold, constant-flux setting.

The all-particle cosmic-ray energy spectrum (differential flux of cosmic-ray particles arriving at Earth as a function of kinetic energy E) shows three distinct spectral breaks in the energy range 10^15 eV ≤ E ≤ 10^18 eV. In order of increasing energy these are called:
  (i) the "knee", a steepening of the spectrum near E ≈ 3 × 10^15 eV;
  (ii) the "second knee" (also called the "iron knee"), a further steepening near E ≈ 10^17 eV;
  (iii) the "ankle", a hardening of the spectrum near E ≈ 5 × 10^18 eV.
At sufficiently low energies cosmic rays are believed to be produced by sources within the Milky Way ("Galactic" cosmic rays), while at the highest energies they are believed to originate outside the Galaxy ("extragalactic" cosmic rays). Candidate Galactic accelerators include supernova remnants (SNRs), whose maximum achievable particle energy E_max is set by the acceleration mechanism (e.g. diffusive shock acceleration) and the source environment. Spectral features can in principle arise either from such source-imposed maximum energies or from changes in cosmic-ray propagation through the Galaxy (e.g. energy-dependent diffusion or escape from the Galactic magnetic field).

Determine:
  (a) the physical mechanism responsible for each of the knee, second knee/iron knee, and ankle features — in particular, decide whether the knee is produced by an energy-dependent change in Galactic propagation, by the maximum energy attainable by Galactic sources such as SNRs (and whether SNRs can in fact accelerate protons up to ≈ 3 PeV), or by some other mechanism;
  (b) at which of these three features the transition from a predominantly Galactic to a predominantly extragalactic component of the cosmic-ray flux begins.

Ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays (UHECRs) are charged nuclei with energies above roughly 10^18 eV. As they propagate through intergalactic space they interact with extragalactic background photon fields (the cosmic microwave background and the extragalactic background light), producing neutral secondary messengers — cosmogenic neutrinos and cosmogenic gamma rays — via processes such as photo-pion production on protons, photo-disintegration of heavier nuclei, and subsequent decays of the resulting unstable particles. The expected diffuse fluxes of these cosmogenic neutrinos and gamma rays at Earth depend on a multi-dimensional space of astrophysical and particle-physics parameters, including:

(a) the chemical composition of UHECRs injected at their sources (the mix of nuclear species and the spectral indices of each component);
(b) the maximum rigidity or maximum energy attainable at the sources;
(c) the cosmological source-evolution model, i.e. the comoving source density as a function of redshift z;
(d) the structure and strength of intergalactic and Galactic magnetic fields, which affect propagation horizons and arrival directions;
(e) the choice of hadronic interaction model used to describe air-shower observables and to constrain (a)–(c) from UHECR data.

The problem is the following:

(i) Determine, as a function of energy E_ν (for neutrinos) and E_γ (for gamma rays), the all-flavor cosmogenic neutrino flux Φ_ν(E_ν) and the diffuse cosmogenic gamma-ray flux Φ_γ(E_γ) expected at Earth, scanning over the entire region of parameter space (a)–(e) that is currently allowed by measurements of the UHECR energy spectrum and mass composition. Provide the full envelope of predictions together with a quantitative characterization of the uncertainties induced by each ingredient, in particular the magnetic-field configurations and the hadronic-interaction models.

(ii) Identify which observable features of Φ_ν(E_ν) and Φ_γ(E_γ) (e.g. peak energy, normalization, spectral shape, flavor ratios, anisotropies, correlations between the two channels) are diagnostic of the underlying UHECR source population, and quantify what can be inferred from a measurement of these fluxes about the sources of UHECRs — including their chemical composition, maximum energy, and cosmological evolution.

For ψ ∈ L²(ℝ), define its Fourier transform on L¹(ℝ) ∩ L²(ℝ) by ψ̂(ξ) = ∫_ℝ ψ(x)e^{−2πixξ} dx and extend to L²(ℝ). A function ψ ∈ L²(ℝ) is called an orthonormal wavelet if the system { 2^{j/2} ψ(2^j x − k) : j, k ∈ ℤ } is an orthonormal basis of L²(ℝ).

For an orthonormal wavelet ψ, define its wavelet dimension function D_ψ : ℝ → ℕ ∪ {0} by
   D_ψ(ξ) = Σ_{j=1}^{∞} Σ_{k∈ℤ} |ψ̂(2^j(ξ + k))|².

Let D : ℝ → ℕ ∪ {0} be any function satisfying the following four conditions:
 (D1) D is measurable and 1-periodic;
 (D2) D(ξ) + D(ξ + 1/2) = D(2ξ) + 1 for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ;
 (D3) Σ_{k∈ℤ} 1_Δ(ξ + k) ≥ D(ξ) for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ, where Δ = { ξ ∈ ℝ : D(2^{−j} ξ) ≥ 1 for all j ∈ ℕ ∪ {0} };
 (D4) liminf_{j→∞} D(2^{−j} ξ) ≥ 1 for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ.

Question: Is the set
   { ψ ∈ L²(ℝ) : ψ is an orthonormal wavelet and D_ψ = D }
path-connected with respect to the L²(ℝ) norm?

Let $n\geq 1$, let $A\in\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$, and let $\Gamma\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a full-rank lattice. A measurable set $W\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ is called an $(A,\Gamma)$-wavelet set if both of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(t)] the translates $\{W+\gamma\}_{\gamma\in\Gamma}$ form a partition of $\mathbb{R}^n$ modulo Lebesgue-null sets;
\item[(d)] the dilates $\{A^j W\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}$ form a partition of $\mathbb{R}^n$ modulo Lebesgue-null sets.
\end{enumerate}
Give a complete characterization of those pairs $(A,\Gamma)$ for which an $(A,\Gamma)$-wavelet set exists.

Let $k$ be a field. A connected graded $k$-algebra $A=\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}A_n$ with $A_0=k$ and each $A_n$ finite-dimensional over $k$ is called Artin–Schelter regular (AS-regular) of dimension $d$ if it satisfies:

(i) $A$ has finite global dimension $d<\infty$;
(ii) $A$ has finite Gelfand–Kirillov dimension, i.e., there is a constant $c$ such that $\dim_k(A_0\oplus A_1\oplus\cdots\oplus A_n)\leq c\,n^c$ for all $n\geq 1$;
(iii) $A$ is Gorenstein, meaning $\operatorname{Ext}^i_A(k,A)=0$ for $i\neq d$ and $\operatorname{Ext}^d_A(k,A)\cong k$ (up to a graded shift), where $k$ is the trivial graded left $A$-module $A/A_{\geq 1}$.

Question: Must every Artin–Schelter regular algebra $A$ be noetherian (i.e., must every left ideal and every right ideal of $A$ be finitely generated)?

Consider a magnetized, weakly collisional plasma described by the Vlasov–Maxwell–Landau system, in which the distribution function f_s(x,v,t) of each species s evolves under self-consistent electromagnetic fields with a small collisional dissipation. In such systems, the relevant invariant of the collisionless dynamics is the (generalized) free energy

  W = ∑_s T_s ∫ ⟨δf_s²⟩/(2 f_{0s}) d³x d³v + ∫ (|δE|² + |δB|²)/(8π) d³x,

which, in the presence of turbulence, is transported nonlinearly through both configuration space (across spatial scales k_⊥, k_∥) and velocity space (toward fine structure in v, characterized by Hermite/Laguerre wavenumber p). At sub-ion scales, several collisionless processes may operate simultaneously and compete: linear and nonlinear Landau damping, cyclotron damping, stochastic ion heating, magnetic reconnection, kinetic Alfvén-wave cascades, whistler cascades, and entropy/phase-mixing cascades.

Provide a quantitative theory of the phase-space cascade of free energy in a 3D-3V driven, weakly collisional, magnetized turbulence that:
(i) specifies how the free-energy flux is partitioned among the competing collisionless channels at ion and electron scales;
(ii) predicts the joint spectrum of free energy E(k_⊥, k_∥, p) in the inertial range between ion and electron gyroscales, including the relative weights of perpendicular spatial mixing and velocity-space (Hermite) phase mixing;
(iii) determines the species-resolved heating rates Q_i and Q_e produced by the cascade as functions of plasma parameters (β_i, T_i/T_e, k_⊥ρ_i);
(iv) characterizes the conditions under which nonlinear advection suppresses ("fluidizes") linear phase mixing versus those under which a genuine velocity-space cascade dominates.

Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional affine Poisson manifold equipped with a Poisson bi-vector $P$, so that the Schouten bracket $[\![P,P]\!]$ vanishes. Let $\partial_P = [\![P,\cdot]\!]$ denote the Poisson coboundary on multivector fields. An infinitesimal symmetry of the Poisson structure is a multivector field $Q(P)$, depending on $P$, satisfying $[\![P,Q(P)]\!] = 0$ modulo terms proportional to $[\![P,P]\!]$. Such a symmetry is called:

- trivial in Poisson cohomology if $Q(P) = [\![P,X(P)]\!]$ for some vector field $X(P)$, again up to terms proportional to $[\![P,P]\!]$;
- nonlinear if its expression in terms of $P$ is of degree $\geq 2$;
- proper if it is not the restriction of a known universal construction giving a trivial class in a generic situation.

(I) Determine whether there exists an infinitesimal symmetry $Q(P)$ of a Poisson bi-vector $P$ on some finite-dimensional affine Poisson manifold which is simultaneously nonlinear, proper, and $\partial_P$-nontrivial.

(II) If no such $Q(P)$ exists, identify the mechanism that forces $\partial_P$-triviality of all universal Poisson cocycles produced from $\partial_P$-nontrivial cocycles, in particular those obtained by applying the graph orientation morphism $\mathcal{O}\to r$ to nontrivial cocycles $\gamma \in \ker d$ in the Kontsevich graph complex.

Consider a fully decentralized learning setting in which a finite set of client nodes are connected via a (possibly time-varying) communication graph, with no central coordinator or parameter server. Each client i holds a local data distribution D_i and a local loss f_i(w) = E_{z~D_i}[ℓ(w; z)], where ℓ is the per-example loss of a (non-convex) deep model parameterized by w ∈ R^d. The global objective is

    F(w) = (1/n) Σ_{i=1}^n f_i(w).

Clients perform stochastic-gradient-based updates and exchange messages only with their current graph neighbors. The protocol must operate under the following adversarial conditions:
 (a) clients may be temporarily unavailable, drop out permanently, or join during execution;
 (b) messages between neighbors may be lost, delayed, or arrive out of order;
 (c) the local distributions {D_i} are non-IID across clients;
 (d) clients perform local updates at heterogeneous and possibly time-varying frequencies;
 (e) the underlying communication topology may be chosen or may change over time.

Design a fully decentralized stochastic-gradient algorithm for training deep (non-convex) models in this setting and prove that it converges to a stationary point of F under conditions (a)–(e), with provable robustness guarantees and competitive practical (wall-clock) convergence time. In particular, characterize the trade-offs between dropout/joining rates, message-loss probabilities, degree of data heterogeneity, update-frequency disparity, and the chosen communication topology, and determine to what extent these can be simultaneously tolerated without degrading convergence.

Consider a federated learning setting in which a central server seeks to estimate the average of model updates $x_1,\dots,x_n \in \mathbb{R}^d$ held by $n$ clients, subject to the following constraints:

1. **Secure aggregation.** The server must learn only the aggregate $\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ (or its average), and nothing about any individual $x_i$ beyond what is implied by the sum. This is typically achieved by cryptographic protocols (e.g., based on additive masking over a finite group) that require client messages to lie in a fixed finite Abelian group and to be combined by group addition.

2. **Differential privacy.** The released aggregate must satisfy $(\varepsilon,\delta)$-differential privacy with respect to the change of any single client's contribution. This is typically achieved by adding calibrated random noise (e.g., Gaussian or discrete Gaussian) either by the server or distributedly by the clients.

3. **Communication compression.** Each client must transmit a compressed/quantized representation $C(x_i)$ of $x_i$ using $b \ll d \cdot 32$ bits, such that the resulting estimator of $\frac{1}{n}\sum_i x_i$ is unbiased (or has controlled bias) and has mean-squared error decaying appropriately in $n$, $d$, and $b$.

**Problem.** Design a family of compression operators $\{C_i\}_{i=1}^n$ together with a secure-aggregation-compatible encoding and a noise-injection mechanism such that:

(a) the compressed messages take values in a finite Abelian group and are combined by the secure aggregation protocol;

(b) the post-aggregation output achieves $(\varepsilon,\delta)$-differential privacy with provable guarantees that account for the compression-induced discretization;

(c) the overall estimator achieves a near-optimal trade-off between communication cost (bits per client), privacy parameters $(\varepsilon,\delta)$, and estimation error;

and prove matching upper and lower bounds characterizing the achievable region of (communication, privacy, accuracy) triples for mean estimation under simultaneous secure aggregation and differential privacy constraints.

Background. Consider the following iterative noisy optimization scheme on a parameter space $\mathbb{R}^d$. Given a sequence of (possibly stochastic) update maps $T_1,\dots,T_K$ derived from per-user loss functions $f_1,\dots,f_K$, define
$$ x_{k} = T_k(x_{k-1}) + Z_k, \qquad k=1,\dots,K, $$
where $Z_k\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2 I_d)$ are independent Gaussian noise vectors and only the final iterate $x_K$ is released. "Privacy amplification by iteration" refers to the phenomenon that, when each $T_k$ is a contraction (in particular, when each $f_k$ is convex and smooth and $T_k$ is a gradient step with a suitable step size), the Rényi differential privacy guarantee for $x_K$ with respect to a change in a single $f_k$ improves geometrically with the number of subsequent iterations $K-k$, even though intermediate iterates would only yield a much weaker (single-step) Gaussian-mechanism guarantee.

Part 1 (Non-convex extension). Let each $f_k:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}$ be a (possibly non-convex) $L$-smooth function with bounded-gradient sensitivity, and let $T_k$ be the corresponding noisy gradient step. Determine whether there exist quantitative privacy-amplification-by-iteration bounds for the released iterate $x_K$ analogous to those known in the convex case—i.e., bounds on the Rényi divergence between the output distributions induced by two neighboring sequences of update maps that decay (or otherwise improve) as a function of the number of iterations performed after the differing step, without requiring convexity or non-expansiveness of $T_k$.

Part 2 (Federated protocols with dynamic participation). Consider a server-coordinated iterative protocol in which, at each round $k$, an unknown subset $S_k$ of users is available, each available user independently decides locally whether to participate, and any participating user may drop out mid-round. Design a protocol that simultaneously satisfies:
(i) robustness: the protocol produces a valid output and a valid privacy guarantee under any adversarial choice of $\{S_k\}$ and dropout pattern;
(ii) self-accounting: the server can compute a tight $(\varepsilon,\delta)$-differential privacy guarantee using only information observable during execution of the protocol;
(iii) local participation: the decision of whether to contribute in round $k$ is made by each user locally, without revealing participation to the server beyond what is necessary;
(iv) good privacy–utility trade-off comparable, up to constants, to that of an idealized protocol with fixed participation.

Either exhibit such a protocol with a proof of its $(\varepsilon,\delta)$-DP guarantee and utility analysis, or prove an impossibility result showing that conditions (i)–(iv) cannot be simultaneously achieved.

Let $\mathcal{H}_6$ denote a 6-dimensional complex Hilbert space. A pair of quantum orthogonal Latin squares of order 6 is an arrangement of $36$ normalized vectors $\{|\psi_{i,j}\rangle\}_{i,j=1}^{6} \subset \mathcal{H}_6 \otimes \mathcal{H}_6$ into a $6\times 6$ array such that:

(i) the $36$ vectors form an orthonormal basis of $\mathcal{H}_6\otimes\mathcal{H}_6$;

(ii) for every row $i$, the symmetric superposition $\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\sum_{j=1}^{6}|\psi_{i,j}\rangle$ is a maximally entangled state of the bipartition $\mathcal{H}_6\otimes\mathcal{H}_6$ (i.e., its reduced density matrix on either factor is $\tfrac{1}{6}\mathbb{I}_6$);

(iii) for every column $j$, the symmetric superposition $\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\sum_{i=1}^{6}|\psi_{i,j}\rangle$ is also maximally entangled.

Decide whether such a pair of quantum orthogonal Latin squares of order 6 exists. (Equivalently, decide whether there exists an absolutely maximally entangled state of four six-level subsystems—a state in $\mathcal{H}_6^{\otimes 4}$ whose reduced density matrix on every two-subsystem marginal is maximally mixed—or equivalently a $((4,1,3))_6$ pure quantum error-correcting code, a 2-unitary matrix in $U(36)$, or a perfect tensor with four indices each ranging over $\{1,\dots,6\}$.)

Consider a chemical reaction network (CRN) consisting of a finite set of chemical species $\{X_1,\dots,X_N\}$ and a finite set of reactions $\{r_ho\}$, each reaction $ho$ specified by stoichiometric vectors and by mass-action propensities $w_ho(\mathbf{n})$ depending on the molecular copy-number state $\mathbf{n}=(n_1,\dots,n_N)\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}^N$. The state $\mathbf{n}(t)$ evolves as a continuous-time Markov jump process governed by the chemical master equation
$$ rac{d}{dt}P(\mathbf{n},t)=\sum_ho igl[w_ho(\mathbf{n}- oldsymbol{
u}_ho)P(\mathbf{n}- oldsymbol{
u}_ho,t)-w_ho(\mathbf{n})P(\mathbf{n},t) igr],$$
where $ oldsymbol{
u}_ho$ is the stoichiometric change vector of reaction $ho$. Assume the network is open (driven by chemostatted species) and operates out of detailed balance, so it can sustain nonequilibrium steady states; assume further that the deterministic (large-volume) rate equations exhibit nonlinearity sufficient to produce nonequilibrium phase transitions (e.g., bistability, limit cycles, or genuine bifurcations of the stationary distribution in the thermodynamic limit).

For minimal nonlinear CRNs of this type (such as Schlögl-type models), techniques exist — including WKB/eikonal large-deviation analysis, instanton calculations, spectral analysis of the master-equation generator, and Doi–Peliti field theory — that yield asymptotic expressions for the stationary distribution, the scaling of fluctuations near criticality, and the rates of noise-induced transitions between metastable phases.

Develop systematic mathematical methods that extend such fluctuation analyses to realistic, high-dimensional biochemical reaction networks (for example, signaling cascades, gene regulatory circuits, or metabolic modules performing a specified biological function), so as to compute or rigorously approximate, in the presence of nonequilibrium phase transitions:
(i) the stationary probability distribution $P_{\mathrm{ss}}(\mathbf{n})$ and its large-deviation rate function;
(ii) the scaling of chemical concentration fluctuations and correlation functions near the transition;
(iii) the mean first-passage times between metastable nonequilibrium phases; and
(iv) the associated entropy production and thermodynamic uncertainty relations along rare trajectories.

Provide a framework that is not restricted to one- or two-species toy models and that applies to nonlinear CRNs of arbitrary (finite) dimension carrying out a specified nontrivial biochemical task.

Background and definitions. A pre-NQ manifold is a non-negatively graded supermanifold equipped with a graded symplectic form of degree 2 together with a degree-1 vector field Q whose square need not vanish; when Q² = 0 the structure is an NQ manifold and recovers a Courant algebroid. Pre-NQ manifolds of degree 2 over a smooth manifold provide a local model for the gauge structure of Double Field Theory (DFT): the standard "doubled" Courant-algebroid-like bracket, the strong constraint, and the C-bracket all arise from derived brackets associated with Q.

Topological T-duality (in the sense of Bouwknegt–Evslin–Mathai or Bunke–Schick) associates to a pair (M, G) consisting of a principal torus bundle M → M₀ together with an abelian bundle gerbe G of Dixmier–Douady class h ∈ H³(M, ℤ) a T-dual pair (M̃, G̃) over the same base M₀, together with the correspondence space M ×_{M₀} M̃ on which the pullbacks of G and G̃ become isomorphic. By a "non-trivially fibered spacetime" we mean a smooth manifold M which is the total space of a non-trivial principal torus bundle over a base M₀, equipped with a gerbe G of non-trivial Dixmier–Douady class.

Known global constructions. (i) For geometric T-duality on principal torus bundles, a globally defined pre-NQ manifold encoding DFT has been built on the correspondence space M ×_{M₀} M̃ equipped with the pullback of both G and G̃; this construction takes the topological T-duality data as input. (ii) For DFT on group manifolds, a globally defined pre-NQ formulation has been given. In both cases the correspondence space, or its analogue, is supplied externally rather than produced by the pre-NQ formalism itself.

Problem. Construct, or prove the impossibility of, a single global pre-NQ manifold P assigned to every "T-dualizable background" (M, G) — a non-trivially fibered spacetime M with gerbe G admitting a topological T-dual — such that:

(a) P is built canonically from the data (M, G) alone, without taking the T-dual (M̃, G̃) or the correspondence space M ×_{M₀} M̃ as part of the input;

(b) P globalizes the local pre-NQ model of DFT (i.e., locally on patches it reduces to the standard degree-2 graded symplectic pre-NQ structure used to derive the C-bracket and strong constraint of DFT);

(c) the topological data of the T-dual gerbe G̃ and of the correspondence space are recovered from P (for instance via canonical transformations of the graded symplectic structure or via reductions of P) rather than being duplicated as input;

(d) the construction specializes to the existing global pre-NQ models in the geometric (principal torus bundle) case and in the group-manifold case.

Decide whether such a globalization exists in general, and if so, provide an explicit construction; if not, identify the obstruction.

Consider the inverse problem of inferring the internal structure and thermal evolution of a giant icy planet (taken to be Uranus or Neptune) from remote and in-situ observations. The forward model maps an interior state — specified by radial profiles of density, composition (rock, water/ices, hydrogen–helium), temperature, and rotation, together with a thermal evolution history — to a vector of observables consisting of:

(i) the external gravitational field, represented by the zonal harmonic coefficients J_2, J_4, J_6, J_8, … obtained from spacecraft tracking (e.g., several Juno-like polar orbits);
(ii) the external magnetic field, represented by the Gauss coefficients of its spherical-harmonic expansion;
(iii) the atmospheric composition, in particular the abundances of He, H_2O, CH_4, NH_3 and noble gases, together with key isotopic ratios;
(iv) the bulk rotation rate (sidereal period) of the deep interior;
(v) the Bond albedo A_B; and
(vi) the wavelength-integrated thermal flux F_th emitted by the planet.

It is known that the inverse problem from gravity data alone (or from any single one of the above observables, however precisely measured) is highly degenerate: many distinct interior density/composition profiles reproduce the same J_{2n} to within current and foreseeable uncertainties.

Problem. Devise a quantitative joint-inversion framework that combines all six classes of observations above so as to break this degeneracy for Uranus and for Neptune. Specifically:

(a) Formulate a joint likelihood / Bayesian inverse problem in which interior-structure parameters and evolution parameters are simultaneously constrained by the gravity coefficients, magnetic-field coefficients, atmospheric abundances, rotation rate, Bond albedo, and thermal flux, with a precise specification of the forward operators and noise/observation models.
(b) Determine, for each observable, the measurement precision required so that the posterior on the key interior parameters (core mass and composition, ice-to-rock ratio, depth of the dynamo region, presence and location of compositional gradients, and luminosity history) becomes unimodal and identifiable.
(c) Prove (or give explicit numerical demonstrations of) the claim that no single observable, however accurate, suffices to render the inverse problem identifiable, while the combination above does.

Provide either a rigorous identifiability theorem for the combined inverse problem or an optimal experimental-design prescription that minimizes posterior uncertainty on the interior and evolution parameters of Uranus and Neptune.

Background. The Weihrauch degrees form a lattice obtained from the preordering of (partial multi-valued) functions on represented spaces under Weihrauch reducibility ≤_W. On this structure one considers several natural operators:
 • the binary join ⊔ and meet ⊓ (lattice supremum and infimum with respect to ≤_W);
 • the parallel product ×, taking (f,g) to the function that solves an instance of f and an instance of g in parallel;
 • the finite-parallelization (or "star") operator ∗, where f∗ solves finitely many instances of f in parallel;
 • the compositional product ⋆, where f ⋆ g is the hardest problem Weihrauch-reducible to "first apply g, then apply f";
 • the diamond operator ⋄, capturing finitely-iterated compositions;
 • the implication / residual → (right adjoint to ⋆);
 • the identity degree 1;
 • the (parallelization) jump operator ′, which assigns to a Weihrauch degree f the degree f′ defined by composing f with the limit operator on Baire space.

It is known that ⊔ and ⊓ are first-order definable from ≤_W; that ∗ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ×; that ⋄ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ⋆; and that → is definable from ⋆ and ≤_W.

Question. Is the jump operator ′ on the Weihrauch degrees first-order definable from the remaining structure (≤_W together with 1, ×, ⋆, ⊔, ⊓, ∗, ⋄, →)? That is, does there exist a first-order formula φ(x,y) in this signature such that for all Weihrauch degrees f and g, φ(f,g) holds if and only if g = f′?

Setup. For a real parameter α ∈ (0,1) and a length λ > 0, the middle‑α Cantor set with first construction interval [0,λ] is the compact set K(α,λ) ⊂ [0,λ] obtained by the usual iterative construction in which, at each stage, the open middle subinterval of relative length α is removed from every remaining closed interval (so each closed interval of length ℓ is replaced by two closed intervals of length ℓ(1−α)/2 separated by an open gap of length αℓ).

Let (α_i)_{i∈ℕ} ⊂ (0,1) and (λ_i)_{i∈ℕ} ⊂ (0,∞) be null sequences (α_i → 0 and λ_i → 0), and for each i set K_i := K(α_i, λ_i). Define the sumset
   S := { ∑_{i=1}^∞ x_i : x_i ∈ K_i for every i }
(assumed convergent, e.g. when ∑ λ_i < ∞), and for each z ∈ ℕ define the tail set
   R_z := { ∑_{i=z}^∞ x_i : x_i ∈ K_i for every i ≥ z }.

Recall that a Cantorval is a nonempty compact subset of ℝ that is the closure of its interior, has no isolated points, and possesses both nondegenerate components and uncountably many components that are not isolated; one further distinguishes:
 • an M‑Cantorval — every nondegenerate component has both sides accumulated by other nondegenerate components;
 • an L‑Cantorval — every nondegenerate component has its left side, but not its right side, accumulated by other nondegenerate components;
 • an R‑Cantorval — symmetric mirror condition on the right side.

Problem. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on the parameter sequence (α_i)_{i∈ℕ} and on the tail sets (R_z)_{z∈ℕ} under which the sumset S is, respectively, an M‑Cantorval, an L‑Cantorval, or an R‑Cantorval.

Consider a scalar Riccati differential equation with continuous T-periodic coefficients,
    x'(t) = A_2(t) x(t)^2 + A_1(t) x(t) + A_0(t),
where A_0, A_1, A_2 : R -> R are continuous and T-periodic for some fixed T > 0. A T-periodic solution is a solution x(t) defined on all of R satisfying x(t+T) = x(t). A limit cycle is an isolated T-periodic solution (isolated in the set of T-periodic solutions). It is classical that such a Riccati equation either has a continuum (one-parameter family) of T-periodic solutions or has at most 2 limit cycles.

Provide effective, verifiable criteria — expressed directly in terms of the coefficient functions A_0(t), A_1(t), A_2(t) — that determine which of the following four mutually exclusive cases occurs:
  (a) the equation possesses a continuum of T-periodic solutions;
  (b) it has exactly 2 limit cycles;
  (c) it has exactly 1 limit cycle;
  (d) it has no limit cycles.

Consider primordial black holes (PBHs): hypothetical black holes formed in the early universe, with masses possibly extending well below a solar mass. A black hole of mass M emits Hawking radiation with characteristic temperature T_H = ℏc^3/(8π G M k_B), producing a thermal-like spectrum of Standard Model particles (photons, neutrinos, electrons/positrons, etc.) whose flux and spectral shape depend on M and on possible modifications of the emission rate (for example, near-extremal rotating or charged black holes, or higher-dimensional black holes, in which the effective Hawking emission is slowed or suppressed). For sufficiently small M, the evaporation accelerates and culminates in a final burst ("explosion") on a timescale shorter than current observation windows.

Determine whether next-generation gamma-ray and multimessenger observatories can detect, or place new constraints on, the following Hawking-evaporation signatures of a cosmological population of PBHs:

(a) the 511 keV gamma-ray line arising from annihilation of positrons produced by PBH evaporation in the interstellar/intergalactic medium;

(b) transient very-high-energy gamma-ray bursts from individual low-mass PBHs reaching the explosive late stage of evaporation;

(c) high-energy gamma-ray and neutrino fluxes from PBH populations whose Hawking emission is modified (slowed or suppressed), as occurs for near-extremal or higher-dimensional PBHs, thereby allowing the viable PBH mass window to extend to much lower masses than in the Schwarzschild four-dimensional case.

Specifically, identify the regions of PBH mass M and abundance f_PBH = Ω_PBH/Ω_DM (and, where relevant, spin or extra-dimensional parameters) for which each signature is detectable above astrophysical backgrounds, and the regions for which non-detection would yield improved upper limits on f_PBH.

Consider an observational study designed to evaluate a causal hypothesis by means of an *elaborate theory*: a prespecified, finite collection of distinct predictions $P_1, P_2, \dots, P_k$ about features of the observed data that should hold if the treatment has a genuine causal effect. Each prediction $P_i$ corresponds to a null hypothesis $H_i$ stating that the corresponding feature is absent (i.e., the prediction fails), and is testable by a statistical test $T_i$ producing a p-value $p_i$ on the observed data.

Investigators specify, in advance:
(a) a partial priority ordering on the predictions, reflecting that some predictions are considered more central to the theory than others;
(b) a desire for inferential statements about each individual prediction $P_i$;
(c) a desire for an inferential statement about the overall *fraction of the theory corroborated*, i.e., a confidence statement about the (unknown) number or proportion of the $H_i$ that are false.

Develop a statistical inference procedure that, on the basis of $(p_1,\dots,p_k)$ (or the underlying joint test statistics, allowing arbitrary dependence), simultaneously delivers:

1. A prioritized testing scheme that respects the prespecified priority ordering and controls the familywise error rate at a chosen level $\alpha$ for the global null $\bigcap_i H_i$ and for the ordered subfamilies;
2. Valid individual inferences for each prediction $P_i$ (e.g., adjusted p-values or simultaneous confidence statements for each $H_i$);
3. A simultaneous lower confidence bound, at level $1-\alpha$, on the number (or fraction) of false hypotheses among $\{H_1,\dots,H_k\}$, interpreted as the portion of the elaborate theory corroborated by the data.

The problem is to construct such a procedure with rigorously established type-I error guarantees for all three components jointly, to characterize its power properties, and to determine optimal trade-offs between prioritized testing and inference about the corroborated fraction.

Consider a two-unit parallel repairable system in which unit 1 has an Erlang-2 (or generally Erlang-type) lifetime distribution with phase parameter a_1 > 0, and the system's state at time t is described by the following probability density functions on the indicated domains, with (x,u,t) ∈ [0,∞) × [0,∞) × [0,∞):

• p_{0,i}(x,t), i = 1,2: the joint density that at time t both units are operating, the operating unit 1 is in Erlang phase i, and the age of unit 2 is x;
• p_{1,i}(x,t), i = 1,2: the joint density that at time t unit 1 is operating in phase i and unit 2 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x;
• p_2(x,u,t): the joint density that at time t unit 2 is operating with age u and unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x;
• p_3(x,t): the density that at time t unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x and unit 2 is waiting for repair;
• p_4(x,u,t): the joint density that at time t unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x and unit 2 is in suspended repair with elapsed failure time u.

The rates are: a_1 > 0 (Erlang phase rate of unit 1), a_2(x) (repair rate of unit 1, depending on elapsed repair time x), b_1(x) (failure rate of unit 1's operating life, depending on age x), and b_2(x) (repair rate of unit 2). The dynamics are governed by a coupled system of first-order linear partial differential equations for the above densities, with integral boundary conditions of "supplementary-variable" type, including in particular
   p_{0,1}(0,t) = ∫_0^∞ p_{1,1}(x,t) b_2(x) dx + δ(t),
together with the analogous boundary equalities at x = 0 (and u = 0) for the remaining densities, and natural nonnegative initial data summing to 1. Here δ(·) is the Dirac delta function modelling the system starting in state (0,1) at t = 0. Assume the rate functions a_2, b_1, b_2 are nonnegative, measurable, and bounded.

Questions:

(a) Show that this PDE system is well-posed in a suitable Banach space setting; specifically, prove that it generates a positive C_0-semigroup of contractions and admits a unique nonnegative time-dependent solution whose total probability is conserved (equals 1) for every t ≥ 0.

(b) Determine the asymptotic behavior of the time-dependent solution as t → ∞: in particular, decide whether it converges (strongly, or exponentially) in the underlying Banach space to the steady-state solution corresponding to the eigenvalue 0 of the system's generator.

A numerical semigroup is a subset $\Gamma \subseteq \mathbb{N} = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$ that is closed under addition, contains $0$, and has finite complement in $\mathbb{N}$. For such a $\Gamma$, define:

- the multiplicity $\operatorname{mult}(\Gamma)$ as the smallest positive element of $\Gamma$;
- the embedding dimension $\operatorname{edim}(\Gamma)$ as the cardinality of the (unique) minimal system of generators of $\Gamma$ as a semigroup;
- the set of pseudo-Frobenius numbers $\operatorname{PF}(\Gamma) = \{\, n \in \mathbb{Z}\setminus \Gamma : n + \gamma \in \Gamma \text{ for every } \gamma \in \Gamma \setminus \{0\}\,\}$;
- the type $t(\Gamma) = |\operatorname{PF}(\Gamma)|$.

For integers $e$ and $m$ with $3 \le e < m$, set
$$T(e,m) \;=\; \sup\{\, t(\Gamma) \;:\; \Gamma \text{ is a numerical semigroup with } \operatorname{edim}(\Gamma) = e+1 \text{ and } \operatorname{mult}(\Gamma) = m \,\}.$$

Determine $T(e,m)$ as an explicit function of $e$ and $m$.

Consider a quantum network modeled as a graph G=(V,E) whose vertices represent quantum nodes (end nodes and quantum repeaters capable of entanglement swapping) and whose edges represent physical links along which short-range bipartite entangled pairs can be generated. A multi-hop end-to-end entangled state between a source s∈V and a destination d∈V is produced by generating link-level entangled pairs along a path P=(s=v0,v1,…,vk=d) and then performing entanglement-swapping operations at the intermediate nodes.

For an edge e∈E and a path P, associate the following per-edge and per-path quantities:
(i) the entanglement-swapping time τ(e) and the cumulative swapping time along P;
(ii) the end-to-end fidelity F(P)=∏ f(e) (with f(e)∈(0,1] the per-link fidelity, modified by swapping);
(iii) the achievable throughput T(P) (expected number of end-to-end entangled pairs successfully delivered per time slot), which depends on per-link generation probabilities and on swap success probabilities in a non-multiplicative way once decoherence and memory cut-offs are included;
(iv) the consumption C(P) of entangled resources required to realize one end-to-end pair;
(v) the delay D(P), including waiting times caused by synchronization of link-level entanglements at swapping nodes;
(vi) a resource-competition penalty R(P;{Pj}) reflecting that other concurrent source-destination requests use overlapping edges and memories.

Define an aggregate route cost c(P) as a function of (τ,F,T,C,D,R). The metric c is in general non-additive: c(P1∘P2)≠c(P1)+c(P2). Equivalently, the achievable throughput along a concatenation of two sub-paths is not the sum (or any simple combination) of the throughputs of the sub-paths.

Problem. Develop a principled route-selection framework that, given (G, source-destination request(s), per-edge stochastic models for entanglement generation and swapping, memory decoherence times, and a set of concurrent requests), outputs a path (or a set of paths) optimizing—or Pareto-optimizing—the vector of objectives (T(P), F(P), C(P), D(P)) subject to a resource-competition model R, while correctly handling waiting-time computations at swapping nodes. In particular:

(a) Identify conditions on the cost function c (e.g., monotonicity, isotonicity) under which a generalized Dijkstra-type algorithm provably returns an optimal path despite non-additivity, and exhibit such an algorithm together with its complexity.

(b) When c does not satisfy such conditions, design an approximation or heuristic routing protocol with provable performance guarantees on at least one of throughput, fidelity, resource consumption, or delay.

(c) Provide a method for computing or bounding the expected waiting times at intermediate repeaters under realistic memory decoherence, and incorporate the resulting estimates into the cost.

(d) Extend the framework to concurrent multi-pair requests, giving a scheduling/routing scheme that manages resource competition.

Consider a quantum network in which end nodes share entangled quantum states (e.g., EPR pairs) generated and distributed across intermediate nodes, and in which qubits are stored in finite-capacity quantum memories. The transport layer is responsible for end-to-end delivery of entanglement and quantum data between communicating endpoints. Two physical constraints govern the design of any such layer:

(i) the no-cloning theorem, which forbids creating an independent copy of an unknown quantum state, so that lost or discarded quantum information cannot be regenerated by retransmission of a copy; and
(ii) the difficulty of establishing end-to-end entanglement, which requires successful entanglement generation, swapping, and (possibly) distillation across multiple links subject to decoherence, memory decay, and limited rates.

In current proposed quantum transport-layer protocols, when congestion arises (e.g., insufficient memory, queue overflow, or link contention), entangled qubits and quantum data are discarded — analogous to packet drops in classical TCP/IP. Because of (i)–(ii), such discarding entails irrecoverable loss of resources that were costly to produce.

Problem. Design a quantum transport-layer congestion-control mechanism that:
(a) responds to congestion (memory exhaustion, link bottlenecks, fidelity decay below a threshold) without discarding entangled states or quantum data whenever feasible;
(b) respects the no-cloning theorem (i.e., does not rely on copying unknown quantum states for retransmission);
(c) coordinates with lower layers (link/network) so that already-established end-to-end entanglement is preserved or productively reused (for example via buffering, rerouting, entanglement swapping/relocation, distillation, or graceful degradation of fidelity);
(d) provides provable guarantees on throughput, fidelity, and stability of the resulting protocol under realistic models of memory decoherence and stochastic entanglement generation.

Formulate such a protocol and analyze its performance, or prove fundamental impossibility bounds showing the extent to which discarding of entangled states is unavoidable under congestion.

Let $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}}$ denote the Lagrangian of the Standard Model of particle physics, formulated as a renormalizable gauge theory with gauge group $SU(3)_C 	imes SU(2)_L 	imes U(1)_Y$, three generations of quarks and leptons, and a single Higgs doublet. In $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}}$ the neutrinos are exactly massless. Experiments observing neutrino flavor oscillations show that at least two of the three light neutrinos have nonzero masses and that the leptonic charged-current mixing matrix (the PMNS matrix) is nontrivial.

Consider extensions $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SM}} + \Delta\mathcal{L}$ that produce three light neutrino mass eigenstates with masses $m_1, m_2, m_3$ and a unitary (or, more generally, possibly non-unitary) leptonic mixing matrix. Standard candidate forms of $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ include, among others:
(i) the Weinberg dimension-five operator $ rac{c_{\alpha eta}}{\Lambda}(\overline{L_\alpha^c}	ilde H^*)(	ilde H^\dagger L_ eta)$ giving Majorana masses;
(ii) type-I seesaw with right-handed singlet neutrinos $N_R$ and Yukawa/Majorana terms $-y_{\alpha i}\overline{L_\alpha}	ilde H N_{Ri} - 	frac12 M_{ij}\overline{N_{Ri}^c}N_{Rj} + \mathrm{h.c.}$;
(iii) type-II seesaw with a scalar $SU(2)_L$ triplet of hypercharge $1$;
(iv) type-III seesaw with fermion $SU(2)_L$ triplets;
(v) inverse and linear seesaw variants with additional singlets;
(vi) radiative (loop-induced) neutrino mass models;
(vii) Dirac-mass scenarios with new chiral singlets and an exact lepton-number symmetry;
(viii) models invoking $R$-parity violation, extra dimensions, or other beyond-Standard-Model structures.

Problem. Determine which choice of $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ is realized in Nature. Specifically:
(a) Is the light-neutrino mass term Majorana or Dirac?
(b) What is the absolute mass scale and ordering (normal vs. inverted) of $m_1, m_2, m_3$?
(c) What are the values of all CP-violating phases in the leptonic mixing matrix?
(d) Identify the underlying high-scale (or low-scale) mechanism of mass generation, and specify a complete set of experimental observables (e.g., neutrinoless double beta decay rates, kinematic endpoint measurements, cosmological mass bounds, searches for heavy neutral leptons, charged lepton flavor violation, non-unitarity tests, oscillation precision data) sufficient to discriminate the realized $\Delta\mathcal{L}$ from all competing renormalizable or effective-theory alternatives.

Let $X$ be a Banach space equipped with its norm topology $\|\cdot\|$ and an additional Hausdorff locally convex topology $\tau$, coarser than the norm topology, such that $(X,\tau)$ is sequentially complete on $\|\cdot\|$-bounded sets and the dual $(X,\tau)'$ is norming for $X$. A family $(T(t))_{t\ge 0}$ of bounded linear operators on $X$ is called a bi-continuous semigroup if:
(i) $T(0)=I$ and $T(t+s)=T(t)T(s)$ for all $t,s\ge 0$;
(ii) $(T(t))_{t\ge 0}$ is exponentially bounded in the norm of $X$;
(iii) $(T(t))_{t\ge 0}$ is locally bi-equicontinuous, i.e., for every $\|\cdot\|$-bounded $\tau$-null sequence $(x_n)$ in $X$ and every $t_0>0$, $T(t)x_n \to 0$ in $\tau$ uniformly for $t\in[0,t_0]$;
(iv) $t\mapsto T(t)x$ is $\tau$-continuous on $[0,\infty)$ for every $x\in X$.

The generator $A$ of such a semigroup is defined via the $\tau$-limit $Ax := \tau\text{-}\lim_{t\downarrow 0}(T(t)x-x)/t$ on its natural domain $D(A)$.

For strongly continuous ($C_0$) semigroups, classical regularization properties include: immediate differentiability ($T(t)X\subseteq D(A)$ for all $t>0$), immediate norm continuity, analyticity, eventual differentiability/norm-continuity, and membership in Gevrey-type classes; each is characterized by spectral/resolvent decay conditions on $A$ and by smoothing of orbits.

Formulate and establish analogous regularization notions (immediate $\tau$-differentiability, eventual $\tau$-differentiability, $\tau$-analyticity, $\tau$-Gevrey classes, immediate $\tau$-continuity, etc.) for bi-continuous semigroups, and characterize each such property in terms of the generator $A$, its resolvent, and the interplay between the norm and the topology $\tau$.

Consider Boolean networks defined as follows. A Boolean network on $n$ variables is a function $F:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}^n$, with $F(x)=(f_1(x),\ldots,f_n(x))$ and each $f_i:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}$. Under the asynchronous update scheme, the dynamics on the state space $\{0,1\}^n$ is given by the transition relation in which, from a state $x$, one chooses any index $i$ with $f_i(x)\ne x_i$ and moves to the state obtained from $x$ by flipping coordinate $i$ (states with $F(x)=x$ are fixed points). An attractor is a minimal terminal strongly connected component of this asynchronous transition graph; an attractor that is not a single fixed point is called complex. A subspace (or subcube) of $\{0,1\}^n$ is a set obtained by fixing some coordinates to $0$ or $1$ and letting the others vary. A trap space is a subspace $S$ that is invariant under the asynchronous dynamics: no transition leaves $S$. A minimal trap space is a trap space that contains no strictly smaller trap space. A motif-avoidant attractor (MAA) is a complex asynchronous attractor whose states do not lie in any minimal trap space.

Provide a rigorous explanation, with quantitative bounds and a computationally efficient characterization, of why motif-avoidant attractors are extremely rare under asynchronous update across the following regimes simultaneously:
(a) Boolean networks arising as models of real biological regulatory systems;
(b) random Boolean networks with sparse in-degree distributions;
(c) random Boolean networks with dense in-degree distributions;
(d) ensembles in which the local update functions $f_i$ exhibit input-node canalization (i.e., for each $i$ there exist inputs $j$ and values $v\in\{0,1\}$ such that $x_j=v$ forces the value of $f_i$).

A complete answer should yield necessary and sufficient structural conditions on $(f_1,\ldots,f_n)$ for the existence of a motif-avoidant attractor that can be checked in time polynomial in $n$ (or otherwise efficiently), together with probabilistic estimates showing vanishing prevalence of MAAs in the random ensembles above.

Background. Let $U$ be a fixed universal prefix-free Turing machine. For a finite binary string $x$, the prefix-free program-size complexity is
$$H(x) = \min\{|p| : U(p) = x\},$$
and the algorithmic probability of $x$ is
$$P(x) = \sum_{p:\, U(p)=x} 2^{-|p|}.$$
A classical theorem of Levin–Chaitin asserts that $H(x) = -\log_2 P(x) + O(1)$.

Consider now Turing machines that perform infinite computations, i.e. computations that never halt but produce (or attempt to produce) an infinite binary sequence on a one-way output tape. For such machines several natural notions of program-size complexity can be defined for an infinite sequence $\omega \in \{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$, for instance:
\begin{itemize}
\item $H^{\infty}(\omega) = \min\{|p| : U^{\infty}(p) \text{ writes out } \omega\}$, where $U^{\infty}$ is a fixed universal machine performing infinite computations;
\item $H^{\infty}_{\le n}(\omega)$, the minimal program length needed for $U^{\infty}$ to output an initial segment of $\omega$ of length at least $n$;
\item the corresponding algorithmic probability $P^{\infty}(\omega) = \sum_{p:\, U^{\infty}(p) \text{ outputs } \omega} 2^{-|p|}$, suitably interpreted as a measure on Cantor space.
\end{itemize}

Problem. Develop the theory of program-size complexity for infinite computations. In particular:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Give a robust, machine-independent definition of program-size complexity $H^{\infty}$ for infinite binary sequences produced by non-halting computations, and identify the invariance properties analogous to those of the classical $H$.
\item Define an appropriate notion of algorithmic probability $P^{\infty}$ for infinite sequences in this setting.
\item Establish the precise quantitative relation between $H^{\infty}$ and $P^{\infty}$, and determine to what extent the Levin–Chaitin coincidence $H = -\log_2 P + O(1)$ extends, fails, or must be modified for infinite computations.
\end{enumerate}

Provide an axiomatic foundation for algorithmic information theory. Concretely: identify a finite list of axioms on a function $C: \Sigma^* \to \mathbb{N}$ (or on a family of such functions indexed by partial-recursive machines), where $\Sigma^*$ is the set of finite binary strings, such that:

(i) the axioms are satisfied by the standard plain Kolmogorov complexity $C(x) = \min\{|p| : U(p) = x\}$ and the prefix-free Kolmogorov complexity $K(x)$, where $U$ ranges over (prefix-free) universal Turing machines;

(ii) any function $C'$ satisfying the axioms is equivalent, up to an additive constant, to the standard Kolmogorov complexity (an invariance theorem of the same type as the classical Solomonoff–Kolmogorov–Chaitin invariance theorem follows from the axioms);

(iii) the principal theorems of algorithmic information theory — including the invariance theorem, the coding theorem, the existence of Martin-Löf random sequences, Levin's symmetry of information, the incompressibility method, and the basic inequalities relating $C$, $K$, and conditional complexities — are derivable from the axioms without reference to a specific model of computation.

Produce such an axiom system, prove the corresponding invariance and structural theorems, and characterize the class of all complexity measures it captures.

Within the framework of a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker cosmology, let the present-day density parameters be Ω_m (matter), Ω_Λ (cosmological constant or dark-energy term in units of the critical density ρ_c = 3H_0²/(8πG)), with H_0 the present Hubble constant. The dimensionless quantity Ω_Λ = Λ c²/(3H_0²) is commonly inferred from the Type Ia supernova (SN Ia) Hubble diagram (apparent magnitude versus redshift), with a fiducial flat ΛCDM fit yielding Ω_Λ ≈ 0.7.

Consider the following potential systematic effects on the SN Ia inference of Ω_Λ:
(a) attenuation of distant SN Ia light by a diffuse intergalactic dust component producing a redshift-dependent dimming mimicking acceleration;
(b) a metallicity dependence of the SN Ia peak luminosity, where progenitor metallicity Z varies with cosmic epoch, producing corrections to inferred distance moduli comparable in magnitude to cosmological effects;
(c) host-galaxy extinction with a non-standard reddening law;
(d) competing cosmological observables, such as the angular-size–redshift relation for ultracompact radio sources, which under certain analyses prefer Λ ≤ 0;
(e) the discrepancy of order 10^{120} between Λ inferred observationally and the vacuum energy density predicted by quantum field theory.

Determine whether, after fully accounting for systematics of types (a)–(d) and reconciling with the theoretical constraint (e), the conclusion Ω_Λ ≈ 0.7 (and in particular Ω_Λ > 0) is uniquely fixed by the data, or whether viable cosmological models with substantially different values of Ω_Λ remain consistent with the full body of observations.

Consider hole-doped perovskite manganites of the form R_{1-x}A_xMnO_3, where R is a trivalent rare-earth cation (e.g., La, Pr, Nd) and A is a divalent alkaline-earth cation (e.g., Ca, Sr, Ba). These materials exhibit colossal magnetoresistance (CMR) and, in a broad regime of doping and temperature, display spatially inhomogeneous "nanocluster" states in which ferromagnetic metallic and charge/orbital-ordered insulating regions coexist on nanometric length scales. Let the tolerance factor be t = (r_A + r_O)/[√2 (r_Mn + r_O)], where r_A is the average A-site ionic radius and r_O, r_Mn are the oxygen and manganese ionic radii; the A-site cationic size variance σ² = ⟨r_A²⟩ − ⟨r_A⟩² provides a measure of quenched chemical disorder.

Address the following:

(a) Determine whether the phase-separated nanocluster state observed in such manganites should be classified as a distinct state of matter, in the sense of having order parameters, correlations, or universal features that are not captured by either of the competing homogeneous phases.

(b) Identify the dominant microscopic mechanism that produces the nanoclusters. Specifically, distinguish among:
 (i) purely electronic phase separation driven by Coulomb-frustrated charge segregation in a strongly correlated electron system;
 (ii) a first-order transition between competing ordered phases that is rounded into a continuous crossover by quenched disorder;
 (iii) elastic/strain-mediated effects arising from local lattice distortions (e.g., Jahn–Teller coupling) and long-range strain fields;
 (iv) any alternative mechanism.

(c) Predict, both qualitatively and quantitatively, how the size, morphology, and volume fraction of the nanoclusters, as well as the relevant critical temperatures (ferromagnetic Curie temperature T_C, charge-ordering temperature T_CO, and any percolative/glassy crossover scales), evolve as the quenched disorder controlled by the tolerance factor t and the A-site size variance σ² is continuously tuned, including the hypothetical limit σ² → 0 with t fixed.

Consider a doped manganite modeled as an inhomogeneous two-phase medium in which, below a characteristic temperature scale, metallic ferromagnetic clusters coexist with an insulating (charge/orbital-ordered or paramagnetic) background. Let p(T) denote the temperature-dependent volume fraction of the metallic phase, with intrinsic resistivities ρ_M(T) for the metallic regions and ρ_I(T) for the insulating regions (e.g. with activated behavior ρ_I(T) ∝ exp(Δ/k_B T) for some gap Δ, and ρ_M(T) showing typical metallic temperature dependence). Assume a percolative transport scenario in which a macroscopic current flows when p(T) exceeds a percolation threshold p_c, and that the assignment of phases to regions of space is governed by an underlying random-field or competing-order free-energy model with parameters that can in principle be extracted from microscopic Hamiltonians for manganites.

Problem. Develop a quantitative theory of phase separation in this setting that yields a closed-form, analytically tractable formula ρ_dc(T) = F(T; p(T), ρ_M(T), ρ_I(T), p_c, …) for the temperature dependence of the d.c. resistivity in the percolative regime, such that:
(i) F is given by simple, explicit functions (not only by numerical resistor-network simulations on a lattice);
(ii) F reproduces the colossal magnetoresistance-type peak in ρ_dc(T) near the metal–insulator transition observed in manganites;
(iii) The parameters entering F are derivable from, or directly relatable to, a microscopic model of competing ferromagnetic-metallic and insulating phases.

State and justify such a predictive formula, or prove that within the percolative phase-separation framework no such universal closed-form expression can capture the observed ρ_dc(T) without resorting to full network simulations.

Consider a classical (or quantum) many-body system whose microscopic dynamics are governed by time-reversible equations of motion (Hamilton's equations, or unitary Schrödinger evolution). Let \(\Gamma\) denote the phase space (or Hilbert space) of the system, and let \(T\) be the time-reversal involution. The microscopic dynamics satisfy: if \(t \mapsto x(t)\) is a solution then so is \(t \mapsto T x(-t)\). Macroscopic observables are defined as coarse-grained functionals \(M_\alpha:\Gamma\to\mathbb{R}\) obtained by partitioning \(\Gamma\) into macrostates, and a statistical description is given by a time-dependent probability distribution \(\rho_t\) on \(\Gamma\), with thermodynamic entropy identified (up to choices) with \(S[\rho_t] = -k_B \int \rho_t \log \rho_t\) or with a Boltzmann-type coarse-grained entropy \(S_B(M) = k_B \log |\Gamma_M|\).

Formulate and justify a general framework — specifying (i) the class of admissible initial conditions (or a measure on them), (ii) the coarse-graining procedure that defines macrostates, and (iii) the mechanism by which microscopic correlations among the dynamical variables decay or are truncated — under which one can rigorously derive, from the reversible microscopic dynamics alone together with these three ingredients, a macroscopic evolution equation for \(\{M_\alpha(t)\}\) and an associated entropy functional \(S(t)\) such that:

(a) \(\frac{dS}{dt} \ge 0\) for all admissible initial data, with equality only at equilibrium;
(b) the macroscopic evolution exhibits a definite arrow of time (Eddington's time-arrow) despite the time-reversal symmetry of the underlying dynamics;
(c) the framework is internally consistent: the assumptions on initial conditions, coarse graining, and correlation damping do not implicitly smuggle in irreversibility, and they reduce to the known phenomenological irreversible thermodynamics (Boltzmann, Onsager, hydrodynamic limits) in appropriate regimes.

Equivalently: identify the minimal additional postulates beyond Hamiltonian (or unitary) microscopic mechanics that are necessary and sufficient to produce the second law of thermodynamics as a theorem about the coarse-grained macroscopic evolution.

Consider a classical many-body Hamiltonian system with phase space $\Gamma$, equipped with the Liouville measure, and a Hamiltonian $H$ generating reversible microscopic dynamics $T_t:\Gamma	o\Gamma$. A statistical state at time $t$ is described by a probability density $ho_t$ on $\Gamma$, evolving via the Liouville equation $\partial_tho_t = \{H,ho_t\}$. Suppose that, prior to time $t=0$, an experimenter performs an external preparation procedure on the system, modeled as a (possibly time-dependent) modification of the Hamiltonian and/or contact with auxiliary systems, after which the system is released to evolve under $H$ alone.

Call an initial ensemble $ho_0$ \emph{relaxation-admissible} if it satisfies the following:
(i) it is supported on a sufficiently large region of $\Gamma$ consistent with the prescribed macroscopic constraints (e.g. energy, particle number);
(ii) it has a comparatively simple functional form, e.g. expressible through a small number of macroscopic Lagrange parameters via a maximum-entropy prescription;
(iii) under the dynamics $T_t$, the macroscopic observables $\{Q_j(t)=\int Q_j\,ho_t\,d\Gamma\}$ exhibit irreversible, asymmetric relaxation toward equilibrium values;
(iv) the relaxation times associated with $\{Q_j\}$ are finite.

Problem. Give a rigorous, constructive characterization of the class of external preparation protocols (acting on the microscopic dynamics in a finite time interval prior to $t=0$) that produce, from an arbitrary pre-preparation state, an initial ensemble $ho_0$ which is relaxation-admissible in the sense above. In particular, identify necessary and sufficient conditions on such preparation protocols ensuring that the resulting $ho_0$ (a) lies in the appropriate maximum-entropy manifold, (b) yields strictly positive finite relaxation times for a designated set of macroscopic observables, and (c) excludes the time-reversed ensembles that would produce anti-thermodynamic behavior.

In constructive quantum field theory, a model is typically analyzed by a convergent multi-scale expansion (for instance a cluster or loop-vertex expansion) that controls the functional integral in a single renormalization-group regime, such as the small-coupling regime in which perturbation theory in the coupling constant g is convergent after Borel summation, or the large-N regime in which expansion in 1/N is convergent for fixed g. For models that exhibit genuinely non-perturbative phases (e.g. asymptotically free theories whose effective coupling becomes large in the infrared, theories with dynamical mass generation, or theories with phase transitions), no single such expansion is uniformly convergent across all scales.

Problem. Develop a rigorous mathematical framework for "gluing" together different convergent expansions associated with different renormalization-group regimes (for example a small-coupling expansion valid at high momentum scales and a 1/N expansion valid at low momentum scales, or vice versa) into a single multi-scale expansion that:

(a) is absolutely convergent uniformly in the ultraviolet and infrared cutoffs;
(b) produces well-defined Schwinger functions satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms (or a suitable Euclidean analogue) for the limiting model;
(c) gives non-perturbative control over correlation functions across the transition between regimes, in particular reproducing the expected non-perturbative phenomena (mass gap, symmetry breaking, or analogous features) of the model.

Concretely, construct such a glued expansion and use it to give a rigorous construction of at least one interacting four-dimensional Euclidean field theory whose existence requires crossing between a perturbative coupling regime and a 1/N (or other non-perturbative) regime under the renormalization group flow.

Let $H = (H_{ij})_{1 \le i,j \le n}$ be a Hermitian random band matrix of size $n \times n$ with bandwidth $w = w(n) \in \{1,\dots,n\}$, defined as follows: the entries $H_{ij}$ with $|i-j| < w$ (interpreted on $\{1,\dots,n\}$, without periodic identification) are independent (up to the Hermitian symmetry $H_{ji} = \overline{H_{ij}}$), each centered with finite variance normalized so that $\sum_j \mathbb{E}|H_{ij}|^2 = 1$, while $H_{ij} = 0$ whenever $|i-j| \ge w$. Consider the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk of the spectrum, i.e. the joint distribution of rescaled eigenvalue gaps near a fixed energy $E$ in the interior of the limiting spectral support.

Prove or disprove the following dichotomy with sharp transition at bandwidth $w \sim \sqrt{n}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $w \gg \sqrt{n}$ as $n \to \infty$, then the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk converge to those of the Gaussian Unitary Ensemble (GUE), i.e. are governed by the sine kernel determinantal process.
\item If $w \ll \sqrt{n}$ as $n \to \infty$, then the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk converge to a Poisson point process.
\end{enumerate}
